    },
    ":question/id": "213b2643-d369-585f-a935-32807ac38964",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-337657",
    ":question/problem": "Calculate the product ${I}_{3}D$ given that ${I}_{3}$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${I}_{3}\\cdot D=$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>{{field-5}}</td><td>{{field-6}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-7}}</td><td>{{field-8}}</td><td>{{field-9}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $D$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and ${I}_{3}$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\cdot D=D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"
  }
